charges and Cartesian coordinates are used as input, and a deep neural network (DNN) is used to exchange information between individual atoms, such that a meaningful representation of their chemical environments (i.e., "descriptor") is learned directly from the data. This approach was first introduced by the DTNN \({ }^{29}\) and has since been refined in other DNN architectures, for example SchNet \({ }^{30}\) or HIP-NN. \({ }^{31}\) The main difference between the two HDNN variants is that descriptor-based HDNNs rely on manually constructed features (e.g., via symmetry functions \({ }^{21}\) ), whereas messagepassing HDNNs learn to construct features in a data-driven manner. Both types of HDNN perform well, but the messagepassing variant is able to adapt the description of heterogeneous chemical environments to the training data, which, depending on the problem at hand, can lead to a better performance. \({ }^{32}\)

The present work introduces PhysNet, an HDNN of the message-passing type designed based on physical principles and inspired by both SchNet \({ }^{30}\) and HIP-NN. \({ }^{31}\) Contrary to existing approaches, interactions between atoms are modeled by learnable distance-based attention \({ }^{33}\) masks biased toward a physically meaningful (exponential) decay behavior. To increase the expressiveness of PhysNet, features of interacting atoms can take separate paths through the architecture. Further, skip-connections, i.e., preactivation residual layers, \({ }^{34}\) are used extensively in the neural network design to refine atomic feature representations. It is shown that PhysNet matches or improves upon state-of-the-art performance on the QM9, \({ }^{35}\) MD17, \({ }^{36}\) and ISO17 \({ }^{30}\) benchmark data sets. Further, two new benchmark data sets are constructed: The first set contains structures probing the PES of \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}} 2\) reactions of methyl halides with halide anions. Capturing the correct long-range physics is particularly important and challenging for these systems due to the presence of strong charge-dipole interactions. Fortunately, NN-based potentials can be augmented by an explicit description of electrostatics, \({ }^{26,37}\) which allows the capturing of such long-range interactions more accurately. By comparing different variants of PhysNet, it is demonstrated that the explicit inclusion of electrostatics in the energy prediction is important for correctly describing asymptotic regions of the PES.

The second data set contains structures for small protein-like compounds (consisting of at most eight heavy atoms) and clusters with water molecules. Available benchmark data sets cover conformational and chemical degrees of freedom, but they usually do not probe many-body intermolecular interactions, which are important for condensed phase systems. Due to their biological importance, proteins in aqueous solution are a particularly relevant system of this kind. However, even small proteins contain hundreds of atoms, which makes \(a b\) initio reference calculations for them prohibitively expensive. Fortunately, it is possible to construct a predictive ML model for large molecules by training on smaller molecules that are structurally similar. \({ }^{20,24,25,38-40}\) These so-called "amons" can be readily constructed by considering a large molecule as a chemical graph, generating all possible connected subgraphs with a fixed number of heavy atoms and saturating the resulting structures with hydrogen atoms. \({ }^{40}\) Due to the fact that most proteins are comprised of only 20 different amino acids, many bonding patterns are shared between proteins, and a comparatively small number of amons is sufficient to cover all possible graphs.

It is shown that a PhysNet model trained on this data is able to accurately predict interaction energies of common side chain-side chain and backbone-backbone interactions in proteins. The model also generalizes to considerably larger molecules than it was trained on: When an ensemble \({ }^{41}\) of PhysNet models is used to optimize the geometry of helical deca-alanine ( Ala \(_{10}\) ), the results are almost indistinguishable from a structure optimized at the reference DFT level of theory (RMSD \(=0.21 \AA\) ). By running unbiased MD simulations on the PhysNet-PES, a "wreath-shaped" configuration of Ala \({ }_{10}\) is found, which, according to \(a b\) initio calculations, is as stable as the helical form in gas phase (the wreath-shaped form is even slightly lower in energy by \(0.46 \mathrm{kcal} \mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) ).

In section 2, the PhysNet architecture and the process used for generating the reference data for \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}} 2\) reactions and solvated protein fragments are described in detail. The performance of PhysNet on these data sets and other commonly used quantum chemical benchmarks is reported in section 3. Further, the generalization capabilities of the model are explored by applying it to \(\mathrm{Ala}_{10}\). Finally, the results are discussed and summarized in section 4.

\section*{2. METHODS}

In section 2.1, the building blocks of PhysNet and its complete architecture are described. Further, the procedure used for training PhysNet on reference data (how the neural network parameters are fitted) is described in section 2.2. The generation of the data sets for \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}} 2\) reactions and solvated protein fragments is detailed in section 2.3.
2.1. Neural Network. The basic building blocks of every fully connected NN are so-called dense layers. They take an input vector \(\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text {in }}}\) and return a vector \(\mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text {out }}}\) according to the transformation
\[
\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{W x}+\mathbf{b}
\]
where \(\mathbf{W} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text {out }} \times n_{\text {in }}}\) and \(\mathbf{b} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text {out }}}\) are learnable parameters, and \(n_{\text {in }}\) and \(n_{\text {out }}\) denote the dimensionality of input and output vectors, respectively. Note that a single dense layer can only represent a linear transformation from input to output. In order to model arbitrary nonlinear relationships, at least two dense layers need to be stacked and combined with a (nonlinear) activation function \(\sigma\), i.e.
\[
\mathbf{y}=\mathbf{W}_{2} \sigma\left(\mathbf{W}_{1} \mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}_{1}\right)+\mathbf{b}_{2}
\]

Throughout this work, the notation \(\sigma(\mathbf{x})\) means that a scalar function \(\sigma\) is applied to a vector \(\mathbf{x}\) entrywise, i.e., \(\sigma(\mathbf{x})=\left[\sigma\left(x_{1}\right) \cdots \sigma\left(x_{n_{\mathrm{in}}}\right)\right]^{\mathrm{T}}\).

Two dense layers combined according to eq 2 can already approximate any (nonlinear) mapping between input \(\mathbf{x}\) and output \(\mathbf{y}\), provided that the first ("hidden") layer is "wide" enough (contains sufficiently many neurons) and an appropriate activation function \(\sigma\) is used. \({ }^{15}\) Many different choices for \(\sigma\) are possible, \({ }^{42}\) including \(\tanh (x)^{15}\) or \(\max (0, x)\). \({ }^{43}\) In this work, the shifted softplus function \({ }^{30}\) given by \(\sigma(x)= \log \left(e^{x}+1\right)-\log (2)\) is used.

While shallow neural networks composed of just two dense layers (eq 2) are already capable of approximating arbitrary functions, \({ }^{42}\) deep neural networks composed of more than two layers were shown to be exponentially more parameter efficient in their approximation capability. \({ }^{44}\)

Here, several reusable building blocks are combined in a modular fashion to form a deep neural network architecture,

