which predicts atomic contributions to properties (such as energy) of a chemical system composed of \(N\) atoms based on atomic features \(\mathbf{x}_{i} \in \mathbb{R}^{F}\) (here, \(F\) denotes the dimensionality of the feature space). The features simultaneously encode information about nuclear charge \(Z\) and the local atomic environment of each atom \(i\) and are constructed by iteratively refining an initial representation depending solely on \(Z_{i}\) through coupling with the feature vectors \(\mathbf{x}_{j}\) of all atoms \(j \neq i\) within a cutoff radius \(r_{\text {cut }}\). For example, consider the case of energy prediction for a \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) molecule specified by a set of nuclear charges \(\left\{Z_{1}=6, Z_{2}=1, Z_{3}=1, Z_{4}=1, Z_{5}=1\right\}\) and Cartesian coordinates \(\left\{\mathbf{r}_{1}, \mathbf{r}_{2}, \mathbf{r}_{3}, \mathbf{r}_{4}, \mathbf{r}_{5}\right\}\). In a first step, a set of initial feature vectors of size \(F\left\{\mathbf{x}_{1}=\mathbf{e}_{6}, \mathbf{x}_{2}=\mathbf{e}_{1}, \mathbf{x}_{3}=\mathbf{e}_{1}, \mathbf{x}_{4}=\mathbf{e}_{1}\right.\), \(\left.\mathbf{x}_{5}=\mathbf{e}_{1}\right\}\) is assigned to the atoms, where \(\mathbf{e}_{Z_{i}}\) are element-specific "embeddings". In subsequent steps, structural information is encoded into the features by coupling them with each other based on learnable functions of the pairwise distances \(r_{i j}=\left\|\mathbf{r}_{i}-\mathbf{r}_{j}\right\|\) between atoms. The final features contain information about each element and its chemical environment and are used to predict a set of atomic energy contributions \(\left\{E_{1}, E_{2}, E_{3}, E_{4}, E_{5}\right\}\) by linear regression. The prediction of the total energy is then obtained by summation of these atomic contributions. Note that because only pairwise distances are used to encode structural information, all symmetries of the
\(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) molecule are automatically encoded in the feature vectors, i.e., for the equilibrium geometry, \(\mathbf{x}_{2}=\mathbf{x}_{3}=\mathbf{x}_{4}=\mathbf{x}_{5}\).

In the following, the individual building blocks of the neural network and important underlying concepts are described in more detail. The complete architecture is schematically represented in Figure 1. Note that for the remainder of this section, superscripts \(l\) are used to denote features or parameters of layer l. All models are implemented in the TensorFlow \({ }^{45}\) framework.

Embedding Layer. An embedding is a mapping from a discrete object to a vector of real numbers. For example, word embeddings \({ }^{46}\) find widespread use in the field of natural language processing, where semantically similar words are mapped such that they appear close to each other in the embedding space. In this work, atomic numbers \(Z \in \mathbb{N}\) are mapped to embeddings \(\mathbf{e}_{Z} \in \mathbb{R}^{F}\), where the entries of the embedding vectors \(\mathbf{e}_{Z}\) are learnable parameters. The embedding layer (purple box in Figure 1A) initializes the atomic features of an atom with nuclear charge \(Z\) to the corresponding embedding vector \(\mathbf{e}_{Z}\) (eq 3).
\[
\mathbf{x}_{i}^{0}=\mathbf{e}_{Z_{i}}
\]

The output of the embedding layer is passed to a stack of \(N_{\text {module }}\) modules sharing the same composition but not parameters.

Module. Each module (see Figure 1B) in the stack (except for the first one, which receives its input from the embedding layer) takes the output of the previous module and couples the features \(\mathbf{x}_{i}\) of each atom \(i\) with the features \(\mathbf{x}_{j}\) of all atoms \(j\) within the cutoff distance \(r_{\text {cut }}\) through an interaction block (see Figure 1C). The features are then further refined atom-wise through \(N_{\text {residual }}^{\text {atomic }}\) residual blocks. Subsequently, the computation splits into two branches: One branch passes the atomic features onward to the next module in the stack (if present) without further modification, whereas the other branch passes the features to an output block, which computes the module's contribution to the final prediction. The split into two branches helps to decouple the feature representations passed between modules from the prediction task at hand.

Residual Block. The ability of a neural network to model arbitrary functions should always increase, or at least remain the same, when the depth (i.e., the number of dense layers stacked on top of each other) is increased, as additional layers could in principle always reduce to the identity mapping and should therefore never decrease the performance. However, this is not observed in practice: As neural networks get deeper, they become increasingly difficult to train because of the vanishing gradients problem, \({ }^{47}\) which leads to a degradation of their performance. In order to alleviate this, it was proposed to add shortcut connections to the neural network architecture that skip one or several layers, creating a so-called residual block. \({ }^{48}\)

Since their introduction, the design of residual blocks was further refined to allow completely unhindered gradient flow through all layers of a neural network. \({ }^{34}\) It was shown that stacking these so-called preactivation residual blocks allows successfully training neural networks more than 1000 layers deep. \({ }^{34}\) In this work, preactivation residual blocks (see Figure 1E) are used extensively to refine the atomic features according to
\[
\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l+2}=\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l}+\mathbf{W}^{l+1} \sigma\left(\mathbf{W}^{l} \sigma\left(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l}\right)+\mathbf{b}^{l}\right)+\mathbf{b}^{l+1}
\]
where \(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l}\) and \(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l+2}\) are input and output features, respectively, and \(\mathbf{W}^{l}, \mathbf{W}^{l+1} \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times F}\) and \(\mathbf{b}^{l}, \mathbf{b}^{l+1} \in \mathbb{R}^{F}\) are learnable parameters.

Interaction Block. In order to predict properties that depend on the environment of an atom \(i\), it is important to model interactions with its surrounding atoms \(j\) in a chemically and physically meaningful manner (Figure 1C). In doing so, it is crucial that all known invariances of the property of interest are respected: For example, the energy of a molecular system is known to be invariant with respect to translation, rotation, and permutation of equivalent atoms. \({ }^{21}\) Further, since it is a valid assumption that most (but not all) chemical interactions are inherently short-ranged, it is meaningful to introduce a cutoff radius \(r_{\text {cut }}{ }^{20}\) such that only interactions within the local environment of an atom are considered. Apart from encoding chemical knowledge directly in the modeling of interactions, this approach has the important computational advantage of making predictions scale linearly with system size.

Based on these design principles, the feature vector \(\mathbf{x}\) of an atom is refined by interacting with its local environment through a "message" \({ }^{28} \mathbf{v} \in \mathbb{R}^{F}\) according to
\[
\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l+1}=\mathbf{u}^{l} \circ \mathbf{x}_{i}^{l}+\mathbf{W}^{l} \sigma\left(\mathbf{v}_{i}^{l}\right)+\mathbf{b}^{l}
\]
where \(\mathbf{u}^{l}, \mathbf{b}^{l} \in \mathbb{R}^{F}\), and \(\mathbf{W}^{l} \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times F}\) are learnable parameters, and "o" denotes the Hadamard (entrywise) product (see Figure 1C). The learnable gating vector \(\mathbf{u}\), inspired by the gated recurrent unit, \({ }^{49}\) allows individual entries of the feature vector to be damped or reinforced during the update. The final message \(\mathbf{v}\) used in eq 5 is obtained by passing a proto-message \(\tilde{\mathbf{v}}\) through \(N_{\text {residual }}^{\text {interaction }}\) residual blocks (eq 4), where \(\tilde{\mathbf{v}}\) is given by
\[
\tilde{\mathbf{v}}_{i}^{l}=\sigma\left(\mathbf{W}_{\mathbf{I}}^{l} \sigma\left(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l}\right)+\mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{I}}^{l}\right)+\sum_{j \neq i} \mathbf{G}^{l} \mathbf{g}\left(r_{i j}\right) \circ \sigma\left(\mathbf{W}_{\mathbf{J}}^{l} \sigma\left(\mathbf{x}_{j}^{l}\right)+\mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{J}}^{l}\right)
\]
and \(\mathbf{W}_{\mathbf{I}}^{l}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathbf{J}}^{l} \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times F}, \mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{I}}^{l}, \mathbf{b}_{\mathbf{J}}^{l} \in \mathbb{R}^{F}\), and \(\mathbf{G}^{l} \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times K}\) are learnable parameters, and \(r_{i j}\) denotes the Euclidean distance between atoms \(i\) and \(j\). The vector \(\mathbf{g}\left(r_{i j}\right)=\left[g_{1}\left(r_{i j}\right) \cdots g_{K}\left(r_{i j}\right)\right]^{T}\) is composed of the values of \(K\) radial basis functions of the form
\[
g_{k}\left(r_{i j}\right)=\phi\left(r_{i j}\right) \cdot \exp \left(-\beta_{k}\left(\exp \left(-r_{i j}\right)-\mu_{k}\right)^{2}\right)
\]
where \(\mu_{k}, \beta_{k} \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}\) are learnable parameters that specify center and width of \(g_{k}\left(r_{i j}\right)\), respectively, and \(\phi\left(r_{i j}\right)\) is a smooth cutoff function given by \({ }^{50}\)
\[
\phi\left(r_{i j}\right)= \begin{cases}1-6\left(\frac{r_{i j}}{r_{\mathrm{cut}}}\right)^{5}+15\left(\frac{r_{i j}}{r_{\mathrm{cut}}}\right)^{4}-10\left(\frac{r_{i j}}{r_{\mathrm{cut}}}\right)^{3} & r_{i j}<r_{\mathrm{cut}} \\ 0 & r_{i j} \geq r_{\mathrm{cut}}\end{cases}
\]
that ensures continuous behavior when an atom enters or leaves the cutoff sphere. The vector \(\mathbf{G}^{l} \mathbf{g}\left(r_{i j}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{F}\) takes the role of a learnable attention mask \({ }^{33}\) that selects different features based on the pairwise distance \(r_{i j}\) between atoms. The attention mask \(\mathbf{G}^{l} \mathbf{g}\left(r_{i j}\right)\) is independent of the nuclear charges of atoms \(i\) and \(j\), as information about their elements is already encoded in \(\mathbf{x}_{i}\) and \(\mathbf{x}_{j}\) (see embedding layer). Note that the Gaussian in eq 7 takes \(\exp \left(-r_{i j}\right)\) instead of \(r_{i j}\) as its argument, which biases attention masks toward a functional form that decays exponentially with \(r_{i j}\) (see Figure 2). Such a bias is meaningful for a chemical system, as it entails the physical knowledge that

