bound state wave functions in two-body systems decay exponentially. \({ }^{51}\) Since only pairwise distances are used in eq 6, the output of an interaction block is automatically translationally and rotationally invariant, while the commutative property of summation ensures permutational invariance.

Output Block. Each output block (see Figure 1D) passes the atomic features through \(N_{\text {residual }}^{\text {output }}\) additional residual blocks and computes the output \(\mathbf{y}_{i}^{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text {out }}}\) of module \(m\) for atom \(i\) by a linear transformation of the activated features according to
\[
\mathbf{y}_{i}^{m}=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}^{m} \sigma\left(\mathbf{x}_{i}^{l}\right)+\mathbf{b}_{\mathrm{out}}^{m}
\]
where \(\mathbf{W}_{\text {out }}^{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{F \times n_{\text {out }}}\) and \(\mathbf{b}_{\text {out }}^{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text {out }}}\) are parameters. How many entries the output vector \(\mathbf{y}_{i}^{m}\) has depends on how many atomic properties are predicted at once. In this work, two variants are considered: The first version predicts atomic energy contributions along with atomic partial charges (i.e., \(\mathrm{y}_{i}^{m}=\left[E_{i}^{m} q_{i}^{m}\right]^{\mathrm{T}}\) and \(n_{\text {out }}=2\) ), whereas the second version predicts only atomic energy contributions (i.e., \(\mathrm{y}_{i}^{m}=\left[E_{i}^{m}\right]\) and \(n_{\text {out }}=1\) ). In principle, other properties could be predicted as well.

Final Prediction. The final atomic properties \(\mathbf{y}_{i}\) are obtained by summing the contributions of the individual modules (see Figure 1A) according to
\[
\mathbf{y}_{i}=\mathbf{s}_{Z_{i}} \circ\left(\sum_{m=1}^{N_{\text {module }}} \mathbf{y}_{i}^{m}\right)+\mathbf{c}_{Z_{i}}
\]
where \(\mathbf{s}_{Z}, \mathbf{c}_{Z} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_{\text {out }}}\) are learnable element-specific scale and shift parameters depending on the nuclear charge \(Z_{i}\) of atom \(i\). The scaling and shifting of the output decouple the values of other parameters from the numeric range of target properties, which depends mainly on the chosen system of units. Elementspecific (instead of global) parameters are motivated by a previous observation indicating that atomic properties of distinct elements can span vastly different ranges. \({ }^{24}\)

The final prediction for the total energy of a system of interest composed of \(N\) atoms is obtained by summation of the atomic energy contributions \(E_{i}\) :
\[
E=\sum_{i=1}^{N} E_{i}
\]

A potential shortcoming of eq 11 is the fact that all longrange interactions contributing to \(E\) beyond the cutoff radius \(r_{\text {cut }}\) cannot be properly accounted for. As long as \(r_{\text {cut }}\) is chosen sufficiently large, this is not an issue. However, in order to account for electrostatic interactions which decay with the inverse of the distance, a large cutoff would be necessary and reduce the computational efficiency of the model. Fortunately, their functional form is known, and they can be explicitly included when computing \(E\). Other types of long-range interactions for which the functional form is also known analytically, for example dispersion corrections like DFT-D3, \({ }^{52}\) can be included as well. Incorporating dispersion corrections into the functional of a ML potential has been proposed previously. \({ }^{26,53,54}\)

Because of the shortcomings of eq 11, for a PhysNet model that also predicts atomic partial charges, \(E\) is calculated by
\[
E=\sum_{i=1}^{N} E_{i}+k_{e} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \sum_{j>i}^{N} \tilde{q}_{i} \tilde{q}_{j} \chi\left(r_{i j}\right)+E_{\mathrm{D} 3}
\]
instead, where \(E_{\mathrm{D} 3}\) is the DFT-D3 dispersion correction (parameters are set to the values of the reference DFT method but can also be learned if no reference values are available), \({ }^{52} k_{e}\) is Coulomb's constant, \(\tilde{q}_{i}\) and \(\tilde{q}_{j}\) are corrected partial charges (see eq 14) of atoms \(i\) and \(j\), and \(\chi\left(r_{i j}\right)\) is given by
\[
\chi\left(r_{i j}\right)=\phi\left(2 r_{i j}\right) \frac{1}{\sqrt{r_{i j}^{2}+1}}+\left(1-\phi\left(2 r_{i j}\right)\right) \frac{1}{r_{i j}}
\]
where \(\phi\left(r_{i j}\right)\) is the cutoff function given by eq 8 . Here, \(\chi\left(r_{i j}\right)\) smoothly interpolates between the correct \(r_{i j}^{-1}\) dependence of Coulomb's law at long-range and a damped term at small distances to avoid the singularity at \(r_{i j}=0\) (see Figure 3). The

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[alt={},max width=\textwidth]{https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/43d3e356-5e34-4d1b-9fa3-9df66a6db96b-05.jpg?height=652&width=847&top_left_y=754&top_left_x=1121}
\captionsetup{labelformat=empty}
\caption{Figure 3. Coulomb interaction. The dotted red curve shows the correct \(\frac{1}{r}\) dependence of Coulomb's law, whereas the solid black curve shows \(\chi(r)\) (see eq 13) for \(r_{\text {cut }}=10 \AA\). Note that for \(r>\frac{r_{\text {cut }}}{2}\), both curves are identical by construction.}
\end{figure}
corrected partial charges \(\tilde{q}_{i}\) are obtained from the partial charges \(q_{i}\) predicted by the neural network (eq 9) according to
\[
\tilde{q}_{i}=q_{i}-\frac{1}{N}\left(\sum_{j=1}^{N} q_{j}-Q\right)
\]
where \(Q\) is the total charge of the system. As neural networks are a purely numerical algorithm, it is not guaranteed a priori that the sum of all predicted atomic partial charges \(q_{i}\) is equal to the total charge \(Q\) (although the result is usually very close when the neural network is properly trained), so a correction scheme like eq 14 is necessary to guarantee charge conservation. Note that the problem of charge conservation has been investigated previously and other correction schemes are possible. \({ }^{55,56}\)
It should be pointed out that the summation over all atom pairs when evaluating the long-range interactions in eq 12 makes the evaluation of \(E\) scale quadratically with system size. Fortunately, various schemes to recover linear scaling are described in the literature, e.g., Ewald summation \({ }^{57}\) or cutoff methods, \({ }^{58}\) and can be applied without modification.

Note that the concept of augmenting a neural network potential with electrostatic interactions is not novel and was first proposed in ref 37 . However, most previous works use a separately trained neural network to predict atomic partial charges, \({ }^{26,37}\) in contrast to the present work, which uses a
single neural network to learn both, atomic energy contributions and partial charges (see eqs 9 and 10). Aside from computational advantages (only a single neural network needs to be trained and evaluated), shared feature representations in such "multitask learning" are believed to increase the generalization capabilities (transferability) of a model. \({ }^{59-61}\)

Apart from allowing the computation of long-range electrostatic interactions, the partial charges \(\tilde{q}_{i}\) can also be used to predict the electric dipole moment \(\mathbf{p}\) of a structure according to
\[
\mathbf{p}=\sum_{i=1}^{N} \tilde{q}_{i} \mathbf{r}_{i}
\]
where \(\mathbf{r}_{i}\) are the Cartesian coordinates of atom \(i\). The ability to predict \(\mathbf{p}\) is useful for example for the calculation of infrared spectra. \({ }^{62,63}\)

Finally, analytical derivatives of \(E\) (see eqs 11 and 12) with respect to the Cartesian coordinates \(\left\{\mathbf{r}_{1}, \ldots, \mathbf{r}_{N}\right\}\) of the atoms, for example in order to derive the forces \(\mathbf{F}_{i}\) acting on each atom \(i\), are readily obtained by reverse mode automatic differentiation. \({ }^{64}\)

Hyperparameters. The PhysNet architecture can be tuned by hyperparameters that control width \((F, K)\) and depth ( \(\left.N_{\text {module }}, N_{\text {residual }}^{\text {atomic }}, N_{\text {residual }}^{\text {interaction }}, N_{\text {residual }}^{\text {output }}\right)\) of the neural network (see Figure 1). Increasing the width and depth of the neural network generally leads to improved performance, but these improvements typically diminish the wider and deeper the neural network already is. Since an increased width and depth is also associated with a higher computational cost when evaluating the neural network, a compromise needs to be found. While it would be possible to optimize hyperparameters for individual learning tasks, for example via a grid search, it was found that this is not necessary for good performance across different tasks. For simplicity, all models used in this work share the same architecture with the hyperparameters summarized in Table 1, unless specified otherwise. Larger values of the hyperparameters were found to only marginally increase performance.

