All structures were optimized using the semiempirical PM7 method \({ }^{86}\) implemented in MOPAC2016. \({ }^{87}\) Starting from the optimized geometry, 100 different conformations for each structure were sampled by running MD simulations (at the same level of theory) at a temperature of 1000 K with a time step of 0.1 fs using the ASE. \({ }^{85}\) Structures were saved every 10 steps, and for each of them, reference energies, forces, and dipole moments were calculated at the revPBE-D3(BJ)/def2TZVP \({ }^{52,83,84,91}\) level of theory using the ORCA 4.0 .1 code. \({ }^{88,89}\) The revPBE functional is one of the best performing GGA functionals in the GMTKN55 benchmark. \({ }^{90}\)

While this initial data already covers many different chemical situations, it is not guaranteed that these structures cover chemical and configurational space sufficiently well to account for all situations encountered in MD simulations. For this reason, the initial data set was iteratively augmented using an adaptive sampling method: \({ }^{93,94}\) An ensemble \({ }^{41}\) of three PhysNet models trained (see section 2.2) on the initial data set is used to run MD simulations, and all structures for which their predictions deviate by more than a threshold value (here \(1 \mathrm{kcal} \mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) ) are saved. Discrepancies in the predictions are a strong indicator that the data set used for training the models does not contain sufficient information for a particular conformation. \({ }^{93,94}\) For each structure saved in this process, energies, forces, and dipole moments were calculated with the reference \(a b\) initio method and added to the data set. Afterward, the models were retrained, and the sampling process was repeated. In total, the data set was adaptively augmented in this way for four times, after which significant deviations between the predictions were found to be rare. Note
that the number of structures necessary to sufficiently sample the configurational degrees of freedom depends on the diversity of the system at hand and the temperature at which simulations are performed. Finally, energies, forces, and dipole moments were calculated with the reference method for 10000 structures of 40 water molecules in a spherical arrangement (obtained by running MD simulations with PM7, see above) to include training examples similar to bulk phase water. The final data set contains data for 2731180 structures. Amons with up to eight heavy atoms sample short-ranged covalent interactions relevant for solvated protein systems, whereas longer-ranged interactions within the cutoff sphere are covered by the dimer and water cluster structures.

\section*{3. RESULTS}

In this section, PhysNet is applied to various quantumchemical data sets that all probe different aspects of chemical space (i.e., chemical and/or conformational degrees of freedom). Apart from the well-established benchmarks QM9, \({ }^{35}\) MD17, \({ }^{36}\) and ISO17, \({ }^{30}\) the model is also applied to the two new data sets introduced in section 2.3.

QM9. The QM9 data set \({ }^{35}\) is a widely used benchmark for the prediction of several properties of molecules in equilibrium. It consists of geometric, energetic, electronic, and thermodynamic properties for \(\approx 134 \mathrm{k}\) small organic molecules made up of \(\mathrm{H}, \mathrm{C}, \mathrm{O}, \mathrm{N}\), and F atoms. These molecules correspond to the subset of all species with up to nine heavy atoms ( \(\mathrm{C}, \mathrm{O}, \mathrm{N}\), and F ) out of the GDB-17 chemical universe database. \({ }^{95}\) All properties are calculated at the B3LYP/6-31G( \(2 \mathrm{df}, \mathrm{p}\) ) level of theory. About 3k molecules within QM9 fail a geometric consistency check or are difficult to converge \({ }^{35}\) and are commonly removed from the data set. \({ }^{28,30,31,96}\) Since the QM9 contains only equilibrium geometries, the benchmark probes just chemical (but no conformational) degrees of freedom.
